## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced DP: partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums and both memoized and tabulated solutions. Demonstrates recurrence derivation, base cases, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Time: O(N^2 K); Space: O(NK) (or O(N))
- Edge-case handling for N < K

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, K){
  const N = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<N;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++) dp[i][1]=(pref[i]-pref[0])**2;
  for(let k=2;k<=K;k++){
    for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
      for(let j=k-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + (pref[i]-pref[j])**2);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][K];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply divide-and-conquer optimization if the cost function supports it?
- How would you adapt for at-most K segments or large N with memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests advanced dynamic programming: partitioning with a non-trivial segment cost that depends on min and max, requiring careful state design and in-loop maintenance of subproblem results. It also validates producing both memoized and iterative solutions and analyzing time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- DP with partition states
- In-loop maintenance of running min and max
- Top-down memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Time complexity: O(D*N^2); Space: O(D*N)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized
function minCost(A, D){
  const N = A.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(null));
  function solve(d, i){
    if (d === 1){
      let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity;
      for (let k = 0; k <= i; k++){ mn = Math.min(mn, A[k]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[k]); }
      return mn * mx;
    }
    if (memo[d][i] != null) return memo[d][i];
    let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity; let best = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      best = Math.min(best, solve(d-1, j-1) + mn*mx);
    }
    memo[d][i] = best; return best;
  }
  return solve(D, N-1);
}

// Bottom-up
function minCostBottom(A, D){
  const N = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(INF));
  // base: d = 1
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++){
    let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      if (j === 0) dp[1][i] = Math.min(dp[1][i], mn*mx);
    }
  }
  for (let d = 2; d <= D; d++){
    for (let i = d-1; i < N; i++){
      let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
      for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
        mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        dp[d][i] = Math.min(dp[d][i], dp[d-1][j-1] + mn*mx);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[D][N-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests interval DP and both memoization and tabulation approaches. It asks for precise state definitions, correct recurrence when ends match, and careful base cases. It also probes understanding of time/space trade-offs for cubic-time DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP on substrings
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Interval DP with matching ends

## Code Example

```javascript
function minPalRemoval(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    dp[i][i] = 1;
    for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){
      dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i+1][j];
      for(let k=i+1;k<=j;k++){
        if(s[i] === s[k]){
          const left = (k-1 >= i+1) ? dp[i+1][k-1] : 0;
          const right = (k+1 <= j) ? dp[k+1][j] : 0;
          dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], left + right);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you count distinct removal sequences?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) per row with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to extend Dijkstra's algorithm to count shortest paths while handling ties and modular arithmetic—a common pattern in competitive programming and graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm with path counting augmentation
- Non-negative edge weights, including zero-weight edges
- Modular arithmetic for large path counts and overflow prevention
- Edge case handling: unreachable destinations, multiple optimal paths, zero-weight cycles

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const MOD = 1e9 + 7;
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);

  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;

  const pq = new MinPriorityQueue({ priority: x => dist[x] });
  pq.enqueue(s);

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const u = pq.dequeue().element;

    for (const [v, w] of edges[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        ways[v] = ways[u];
        pq.enqueue(v);
      } else if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], paths: ways[t] };
}
```

## Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: O((N + M) log N) due to priority queue operations
- **Space**: O(N) for distance and path count arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count distinct second-shortest paths that share subpaths?  
- How does this adapt if negative weights are allowed but no negative cycles exist?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a standard data structure with array-based tree, index math, and clear complexity expectations.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree
- Range maximum
- Point updates
- Space/time complexity

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(arr) {
    this.n = arr.length;
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < this.n) this.size <<= 1;
    this.tree = new Array(this.size * 2).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i < this.n; i++) this.tree[this.size + i] = arr[i];
    for (let i = this.size - 1; i > 0; i--) this.tree[i] = Math.max(this.tree[i<<1], this.tree[i<<1|1]);
  }
  update(idx, val) {
    let pos = this.size + idx;
    this.tree[pos] = val;
    for (pos >>= 1; pos > 0; pos >>= 1) {
      this.tree[pos] = Math.max(this.tree[pos<<1], this.tree[pos<<1|1]);
    }
  }
  query(l, r) {
    let res = -Infinity;
    l += this.size; r += this.size;
    while (l <= r) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[l++]);
      if (!(r & 1)) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[r--]);
      l >>= 1; r >>= 1;
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for range minimum?
- How would you implement lazy propagation for range updates?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates blending two classic sorts to optimize real workloads and caching behavior, plus reasoning about stability and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Hybrid sorting (Introsort-like) with depth-limited quicksort
- Threshold tuning for switching to mergesort
- Stability in final output and how to preserve it
- Complexity: worst-case O(n log n) via depth cap, average-case O(n log n); extra space for merges

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, lo=0, hi=arr.length-1, depth=0) {
  const n = hi - lo + 1;
  if (n <= 32) return stableMergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
  if (depth > 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length))) return heapSort(arr, lo, hi);
  const p = partition(arr, lo, hi);
  hybridSort(arr, lo, p-1, depth+1);
  hybridSort(arr, p+1, hi, depth+1);
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you benchmark the threshold and pivot strategy across diverse datasets?
- How would you adapt for strict in-place constraints and full stability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests mastery of DAG shortest paths and cross-checks understanding of BFS/DFS-derived ordering vs Dijkstra's heap-based approach.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (DFS or Kahn)
- DAG single-source shortest paths via DP
- Edge relaxation and cycle detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function dagSSSP(n, edges, s){
  const g=[...Array(n)].map(()=>[]);
  const indeg=new Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){g[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++;}
  const q=[]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[]; while(q.length){ const u=q.shift(); topo.push(u); for(const [v] of g[u]){ if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);} }
  const INF=1e15; const dist=new Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){ if(dist[u]===INF) continue; for(const [v,w] of g[u]) dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);} 
  return dist;
}
```

